--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertThrows` consumes the throw it matched without discharging the failure that raised it, and `assertCount` names both sides of an empty subject -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed` and `rule:testing/failure-ledger`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5's ledger is what `expectFailure` reads, and `assertThrows` never writes
// to it: matching a throw is not the same act as recording that an assertion
// failed, so an assertion provoked *inside* an `assertThrows` body is still
// owed a discharge afterwards. That is the whole reason a test claiming an
// assertion fails writes `expectFailure` and not
// `assertThrows(..., Core\Test\Failure::class)`, and nothing else observes it:
// both spellings look identical from the throw alone.
//
// The bodies are named static methods so each row runs one piece of code
// rather than a re-spelling of it; a method reference `Class::method(...)`
// does not exist yet, so the `callable` is an anonymous function calling
// one.

final class Ran {
    public static function miscount(): void {
        array<string> $rows = ["a", "b", "c"];
        Core\Test::assertCount($rows, 2);
    }

    public static function parse(): void {
        throw new ParseError("the input is not JSON");
    }

    // A failing assertion, caught by the member that claims it -- the throw is
    // consumed and the ledger entry it wrote is not.
    public static function catchesTheFailure(): void {
        Core\Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Ran::miscount(); }, Core\Test\Failure::class);
    }

    // An ordinary exception, caught the same way. No assertion failed here, so
    // there is nothing in the ledger for anyone to discharge.
    public static function catchesTheParseError(): void {
        Core\Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Ran::parse(); }, ParseError::class);
    }

    // `assertThrows` failing on its own account: the class it was given is not
    // the one thrown, so *it* records a failure, which is an ordinary entry.
    public static function namesTheWrongClass(): void {
        Core\Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Ran::parse(); }, LogicError::class);
    }
}

int $discharged = 0;
int $refused = 0;

try {
    Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Ran::catchesTheFailure(); });
    $discharged = $discharged + 1;
} catch (Throwable $keptTheEntry) {
    echo "the entry was lost: ", $keptTheEntry->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Ran::namesTheWrongClass(); });
    $discharged = $discharged + 1;
} catch (Throwable $ownFailure) {
    echo "the member's own failure was lost: ", $ownFailure->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Ran::catchesTheParseError(); });
    $discharged = $discharged + 1;
} catch (Throwable $recordedNothing) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    echo $recordedNothing->message, "\n";
}

// Two bodies discharged and one refused, counted rather than read off the
// lines above: a member that discharged the entry it consumed would lose the
// first, and one that wrote an entry for every throw it matched would keep the
// third.
echo "discharged=", $discharged, "/2 refused=", $refused, "/1\n";

// § 4's other edge, on the subject `assertCount` is written around: an empty
// array is a length like any other, and the report names both sides of it with
// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2's bag in front, exactly as the predicate members' reports do.
array<string> $empty = [];
Core\Test::assertCount($empty, 0);
try {
    Core\Test::assertCount($empty, 1, {message: "the page holds a screenful"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $short) {
    echo $short->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::expectFailure failed: the callable ran without a failed assertion
discharged=2/2 refused=1/1
the page holds a screenful: Core\Test::assertCount failed: `$actual` holds 0 entries, `$expected` is 1
